  "result": {
    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.from_edges - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.from_edges.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.from_edges()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Build a new graph from an edge list.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.from_edges()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Build a new graph from an edge list.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.from_edges()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Build a new graph from an edge list.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nBuild a new graph from an edge list.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L2504-L2755){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.from_edges(\n    edges,\n    directed=False,\n    weighted=None,\n    nodes=None,\n    graph_name=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Graph from edges](../assets/graphs/from-edges.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: a card with the edge list, four (source, target, weight) tuples: A to B 2, A to C 1, B to D 3 and C to D 4, and below it nodes equal to the list with E, in the accent color. Right: the new weighted graph, a four-cycle A, B, D, C with each weight on its edge, every endpoint made a node, and the isolated node E, which only nodes= names, in the accent color beside it.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThe quickest way to turn a list of pairs into a graph: every endpoint becomes a node,\nin order of first appearance, so there's no need to create the nodes first or to pass\n`auto_create_nodes=True`. It's EdgeCase's version of NetworkX's `Graph(edge_list)` and\nigraph's `Graph.TupleList()`.\n\nThe edge items have the same shapes as in `add_edges()`: `(source, target)`,\n`(source, target, weight)`, `(source, target, attrs)` and\n`(source, target, weight, attrs)`, where a dict's `\"weight\"` key also sets the weight\nand its `\"rel\"` key the relationship type. The rows of a NumPy array work too (as an\narray has one dtype, whole-number floats in its first two columns count as integers).\n\nEvery endpoint is a node *label*, not a node ID: values that aren't strings are\nconverted with `str()`, so `[(0, 1), (1, 2)]` gives nodes labeled `\"0\"`, `\"1\"` and\n`\"2\"` (and `1` and `\"1\"` are the same node). Repeated pairs make parallel edges and\n`(a, a)` makes a self-loop. The whole build is recorded as a single step (see `steps`).\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[edges]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Any]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The edges, as a list (or any iterable) of tuples or lists, or a NumPy array with\n    two or three columns.\n\n<code>[directed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Whether the graph is directed. Default is `False`.\n\n<code>[weighted]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[bool]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Whether the graph is weighted. If `None` (the default), it is when any edge has a\n    weight.\n\n<code>[nodes]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[list, dict, None]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional nodes to create first, in the forms `add_nodes()` accepts: a list of labels\n    or `(label, attrs)` tuples, or a dict mapping each label to its attributes. Use it to\n    add isolated nodes, to give nodes attributes, or to fix the node order (and so the\n    node IDs). Endpoints not listed are added after these.\n\n<code>[graph_name]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional name for the graph.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new graph holding the nodes and edges.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [TypeError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If an edge isn't one of the accepted shapes or has a weight that isn't a number,\n    or a node's attributes aren't a dict.\n\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If an endpoint is `None`, an edge gives its weight twice, or `nodes=` lists a label\n    more than once.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~add_edges`) \n\n:   Add an edge list to an existing graph.\n\n[](`~add_nodes`) \n\n:   Add nodes, with attributes, before the edges.\n\n[](`~add_edge`) \n\n:   Add one edge, with its attributes.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA list of pairs is all it takes:\n\n::: {#90129e8a .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\"), (\"C\", \"D\")]\n)\ngraph\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\nNumber of nodes                : 4\nNumber of edges                : 4\nDirected                       : False\nDensity                        : 0.67\nAverage degree (min, max)      : 2.00 (1, 3)\nConnected                      : True\nNumber of connected components : 1\nTree                           : False\nBipartite                      : False\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nA number as the third element is the edge's weight, and the graph is weighted\nbecause some edge has one:\n\n::: {#b14d68ee .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nroads = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [\n        (\"Oslo\", \"Bergen\", 463),\n        (\"Oslo\", \"Trondheim\", 494),\n        (\"Bergen\", \"Trondheim\", 633),\n    ]\n)\nweights = roads.get_edge_df()[\"weight\"].to_list()\nroads.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True), weights\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(['Oslo--Bergen', 'Oslo--Trondheim', 'Bergen--Trondheim'],\n [463.0, 494.0, 633.0])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nIntegers become labels, and `nodes=` adds a node that no edge mentions:\n\n::: {#76030ebd .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ng = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(0, 1), (1, 2)], directed=True, nodes=[0, 1, 2, 3]\n)\nnodes = g.get_node_df().select([\"id\", \"label\"])\nnodes, g.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(shape: (4, 2)\n ┌─────┬───────┐\n │ id  ┆ label │\n │ --- ┆ ---   │\n │ i64 ┆ str   │\n ╞═════╪═══════╡\n │ 1   ┆ 0     │\n │ 2   ┆ 1     │\n │ 3   ┆ 2     │\n │ 4   ┆ 3     │\n └─────┴───────┘,\n ['0->1', '1->2'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nAttribute dicts become edge attributes, and `nodes=` can give nodes theirs:\n\n::: {#2ebc22a6 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ng = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [\n        (\"ann\", \"bob\", {\"rel\": \"friend\", \"since\": 2019}),\n        (\"bob\", \"cat\", {\"rel\": \"boss\"}),\n    ],\n    nodes={\"ann\": {\"age\": 31}, \"cat\": {\"age\": 45}},\n)\nnodes = g.get_node_df().select([\"label\", \"age\"])\nnodes, g.get_edge_df().select([\"rel\", \"since\"])\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\n(shape: (3, 2)\n ┌───────┬──────┐\n │ label ┆ age  │\n │ ---   ┆ ---  │\n │ str   ┆ i64  │\n ╞═══════╪══════╡\n │ ann   ┆ 31   │\n │ cat   ┆ 45   │\n │ bob   ┆ null │\n └───────┴──────┘,\n shape: (2, 2)\n ┌────────┬───────┐\n │ rel    ┆ since │\n │ ---    ┆ ---   │\n │ str    ┆ i64   │\n ╞════════╪═══════╡\n │ friend ┆ 2019  │\n │ boss   ┆ null  │\n └────────┴───────┘)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### From a bank export\n\nA bank's export of payments is a table with one row per payment. The rows of its first\nthree columns are `(payer, payee, amount)` tuples, which is all `from_edges()` needs for a\ndirected graph weighted by the amount paid:\n\n::: {#8d95eb42 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport polars as pl\n\nexport = pl.DataFrame(\n    {\n        \"payer\": [\n            \"Acme\", \"Acme\", \"Dee\", \"Eve\", \"Dee\",\n            \"Ana\", \"Ana\", \"Ben\", \"Cruz\", \"Cruz\",\n        ],\n        \"payee\": [\n            \"Dee\", \"Eve\", \"Eve\", \"Dee\", \"Grocer\",\n            \"Dee\", \"Ben\", \"Cruz\", \"Ana\", \"Ivy\",\n        ],\n        \"amount\": [2600, 2700, 80, 25, 64, 120, 9500, 9400, 9300, 3000],\n        \"date\": [\n            \"2026-03-01\", \"2026-03-01\", \"2026-03-02\", \"2026-03-03\",\n            \"2026-03-03\", \"2026-03-04\", \"2026-03-10\", \"2026-03-11\",\n            \"2026-03-12\", \"2026-03-13\",\n        ],\n    }\n)\n\npayments = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    export.select(\"payer\", \"payee\", \"amount\").iter_rows(), directed=True\n)\n\npayments.plot(edge_width_by=\"weight\", label_by=\"label\", seed=4)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"500\" viewBox=\"14.0 18.2 677.0 483.6\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"14.0\" y=\"18.2\" width=\"677.0\" height=\"483.6\" fill=\"var(--ect-3239c383)\"/>\n<g opacity=\"0.4\"><line x1=\"187.2\" y1=\"350.5\" x2=\"272.2\" y2=\"315.4\" stroke=\"var(--ect-e5dfd507)\